"""Original complete writing responses; students may produce different valid work."""
COMPOSITIONS=[
'''The envelope had been lying on the classroom floor all morning. I noticed it only when my chair caught its corner. Inside were six hundred rupees and a folded note reading, “For the journey.” There was no name.

For a moment I thought of the science excursion. My own permission form was still unsigned because the bus fare was more than my mother could spare that week. The money would have solved an awkward conversation. I put the envelope on my desk instead.

At lunch I asked the class monitor whether anyone had reported a loss. She shook her head. Two boys immediately offered to claim it, laughing until I asked them what was written on the note. Neither knew. I decided that another general announcement would only attract more guesses.

Our teacher took the envelope to the office and asked me to come back after the last lesson. By then I regretted handing it over. Someone had probably been careless with money they hardly needed, I told myself, while my own form lay useless in my bag.

When I returned, the office assistant was standing beside a younger pupil. The boy’s eyes were red. His grandmother had given him the money for a bus journey to visit his father in hospital. He described the note before the teacher showed it to him. Then he held the envelope with both hands, as carefully as if it might break.

Outside, he thanked me and hurried towards the gate. I watched him go, embarrassed by the story I had invented about the unknown owner.

That evening I told my mother about the excursion and the envelope. We still had to work out the fare; honesty had not magically paid it. But the conversation was easier than I had feared. For once I had brought home a problem without bringing home an excuse.''',
'''The bus stopped just beyond the bridge, and the driver switched off the engine. I looked at my watch. There were forty minutes until my music examination, and the road ahead had disappeared beneath a line of stationary vehicles.

An elderly passenger in the seat opposite began asking questions. How far was the examination centre? Did I have someone to call? Had I eaten? I answered impatiently. His calmness seemed almost insulting when everything important in my day was going wrong.

The driver returned from the front of the queue. A fallen branch had blocked the road. Traffic might move in half an hour, or it might not. I telephoned the centre, but nobody answered. The elderly man opened a small paper map that I had dismissed earlier as an unnecessary possession.

He pointed to a footbridge and a lane through the old market. “It is a walk,” he said, “but it reaches the same main road.” I hesitated. My instrument case was heavy, and I did not know the neighbourhood. He asked the conductor to confirm the route, then offered to walk as far as the market with me.

We moved slowly at first. He stopped at a shop to check the next turn rather than pretend certainty. At the square, a cycle-rickshaw driver agreed to take me the remaining distance. I climbed in, suddenly aware that the man had abandoned his own comfortable seat and added an inconvenient walk to his morning.

I reached the centre with four minutes to spare. My first scale shook, but the rest of the examination passed without disaster.

On the journey home I remembered how sharply I had answered his questions. I had mistaken practical concern for interference and composure for indifference. The most useful person on the bus had been the one I was least willing to listen to. I never learned his name, but I have remembered his folded map whenever another person’s quietness has tempted me to make a quick judgement.''',
'''I had rehearsed my introduction to the science exhibition until I could deliver it without looking at my notes. Every label was straight, every switch worked, and our model was ready before the judges arrived. Then the hall lights failed.

For a few seconds nobody moved. Our electric pump stopped, leaving the demonstration channel dry. I pressed the switch twice, as though determination might supply the missing current. Across the aisle, someone laughed nervously. My partner, Farah, began gathering the visitors around our table.

“We can show you the principle without the motor,” she said.

That had not been part of my introduction. I whispered that we should wait for the electricity. Farah pointed to the clear container above the channel and asked me to hold it higher. When she opened the small outlet, water ran through the model under gravity. Our carefully painted landscape was suddenly doing its work without the equipment on which I had depended.

A judge asked why raising the container increased the flow. I started the sentence I had memorised, realised it did not answer him, and stopped. Farah described the height difference. I added an explanation about the available gravitational potential energy. For the first time that morning, we were discussing the model instead of performing beside it.

Younger pupils asked whether the same arrangement would work in their gardens. We tried different heights, collected the water and repeated the demonstration. One attempt overflowed the channel, giving us an unexpected opportunity to explain the model’s limitations. I crossed out two impressive but empty phrases in my notebook.

The lights returned before the judging ended. We used the pump again, but this time we explained what it changed and what it did not. We did not win the largest prize. We did, however, answer every question ourselves. Packing away the dripping containers, I looked at my unused opening speech and smiled.

For the first time, I was glad that things had not gone according to plan.''',
'''By late afternoon the market seemed to be holding its breath before the festival. Strings of unlit bulbs crossed the narrow lane, and ladders leaned against shutters that would not close until midnight. Even the familiar vegetable stalls had surrendered a corner to flowers.

At the entrance, a woman separated marigolds with quick, practised movements. Orange petals clung to her sleeves. Beside her, a child counted completed garlands and carefully moved one from a heap whenever she lost her place. Customers bent close to inspect the flowers, then straightened into the stream of people before the next bargain began.

The sweet shop was warm enough to cloud its glass display. Inside, trays arrived from the kitchen with tiny metallic sounds as they touched the counter. The smell of hot sugar drifted into the lane and mixed with incense from a neighbouring stall. A boy holding an empty box watched the weighing scales more attentively than any adult around him.

Near the cloth shop, two customers argued over colours that looked almost identical to me. The shopkeeper carried each length to the doorway so that evening light could decide the matter. His assistant climbed a stool, took down another bundle and waited with the patience of someone who had repeated the same movement all day.

The lane narrowed where a delivery cart had stopped. People turned sideways, warning one another about the wheel. An impatient horn sounded from outside the market, but here a raised hand and a brief apology moved traffic more effectively than noise.

As darkness gathered, the first string of bulbs came on. The flowers deepened in colour, the glass counters brightened, and the exhausted faces behind them became easier to see. I had entered looking for decorations. I left noticing the work that made the celebration possible: hands tying, weighing, carrying and counting long after the purchases had become presents in someone else’s house. The market’s excitement was real, but it rested on an immense and mostly unnoticed effort.''',
'''At six in the morning the railway forecourt belonged to people who knew where they were going. Porters pushed empty trolleys across the paving, their wheels rattling in the cool air. A tea seller lifted his first kettle, and the steam briefly hid his face.

I waited on the public bench while my father collected a ticket. Without the evening crowds, the square looked unexpectedly large. A cleaner moved fallen leaves into a neat line. Two commuters stood apart beneath the timetable, already dressed for offices that would not open for hours. Their silence was purposeful rather than uncomfortable.

The shops were closed, but the station was awake. Announcements travelled clearly across the open space. Each arrival produced a short burst of movement, followed by another quiet interval. I could distinguish individual sounds: a suitcase closing, coins touching a counter, a bicycle bell beyond the gate.

When I returned that evening, the bench was almost hidden. People waited beside bags, children circled a pillar, and a driver called a destination until it became part of the general noise. The tea seller no longer needed to raise his voice; customers had formed a patient queue beside his stove.

Light changed the forecourt more completely than the crowd did. Shop signs shone against the darkening sky. Headlamps swept across faces, revealing an expression for a second before moving on. The pale paving that had seemed spacious in the morning became a patchwork of shadows and scattered wrappers.

Yet the same cleaner was there, collecting the day’s remains. I recognised his careful movement before I recognised his face. The square’s two lives suddenly seemed connected by work that continued through both.

In the morning I had thought of the forecourt as a place of departures. By evening it looked equally like a place of reunions and delay. Neither impression was false. The place had not changed its purpose; a full day had simply shown me more of the people who depended on it.''',
'''Behind the curtain, the hall sounded much larger than it had during rehearsal. Chairs scraped, someone tested the microphone, and a brief squeal rose from the speakers. I held the first page of my script even though I had promised myself I would leave it in the dressing room.

Our stage manager raised one finger. A strip of light appeared beneath the curtain. Farah, standing beside me, silently moved her lips through the opening exchange. I wanted to reassure her, but my own mouth had become unhelpfully dry. From the other side came the principal’s final sentence and then applause.

The curtain opened before I felt ready. Beyond the bright lamps, the audience was a dark, uneven shape. I found the chair that marked my starting position and began. My first line sounded too quick. Then Farah answered, exactly as she had in rehearsal, and the scene acquired a rhythm that was easier to trust than my nerves.

Halfway through, a cup rolled off the table. It struck the floor and continued towards the front of the stage. For one terrible second I watched it instead of listening. Farah bent to retrieve it while keeping her character’s irritated expression. A few people laughed. The small accident had become part of the scene.

After that I could hear the audience reacting. Their laughter arrived in places I had expected silence, and their silence made the serious moments feel worth waiting for. The performance was no longer a sequence I had to survive. It was a conversation whose other half sat beyond the lights.

When the curtain finally closed, we remained still until the applause grew unmistakable. Someone behind me whispered, “We did it,” and the tension broke into hurried embraces and relieved laughter.

The hall was still full, but it no longer sounded threatening. I picked up my abandoned script and noticed the damp fold where my hand had held it. The paper recorded a fear that the performance itself had gradually made unnecessary.''',
'''Competition can bring out excellent work, but it does not automatically bring out the best in every student. Its value depends on what is rewarded, how fairly it is organised and what happens to those who do not win.

A clear goal can make practice purposeful. A debate scheduled for a particular day gives speakers a reason to test their arguments rather than postpone preparation. A race allows a runner to compare an actual performance with previous times. When competitors respect one another, each can discover possibilities that comfortable practice alone might not reveal.

Competition can also teach an important discipline: accepting an outcome without making it an excuse. A student who loses and then studies the judge’s comments may learn more than a winner who stops improving. The result is useful when it begins a conversation about performance rather than ending one about personal worth.

However, a school that celebrates only first place creates avoidable damage. Students may choose easy events, conceal mistakes or refuse to help classmates whom they see only as rivals. Unequal access to coaching and equipment can make a supposedly fair contest measure opportunity as much as effort. Repeated defeat without guidance can discourage the very improvement that competition is meant to encourage.

Schools should therefore combine competitive events with shared work. Teams can practise together, recognise personal improvement and discuss mistakes after results are announced. Clear rules and accessible opportunities matter more than elaborate trophies. Teachers should praise preparation, honesty and generous conduct as visibly as they praise victory.

The strongest objection is that such qualifications soften competition. I think they make it educational. A school is responsible for the development of all its pupils, including those outside the prize photograph. Competition brings out the best when winning remains one result of worthwhile effort, not the only proof that the effort mattered. Used with fairness and reflection, it can challenge students without teaching them to measure every person against a rank.''',
'''Every school should reserve regular time for practical community work, provided that the work answers a real need and is organised responsibly. An afternoon each week can be valuable if learning and service are planned together rather than treated as a public display.

Classroom knowledge becomes more exact when students must use it. A group examining water waste has to measure quantities, compare records and explain a proposed repair. Pupils helping at a library must listen to readers and organise information clearly. These experiences connect subjects that can otherwise seem like separate collections of examination answers.

Community work can also correct easy assumptions. Students may discover that a problem already has local experts, that an apparently simple solution is unaffordable, or that the people receiving help want something different from what volunteers expected. Respectful listening is therefore part of the work, not a delay before it begins.

There are legitimate objections. Travel takes time, safety arrangements cost money, and badly managed projects can interrupt lessons while providing little benefit. Schools with fewer resources should not be expected to organise an impressive weekly expedition. Nor should students replace paid workers or undertake tasks for which they are untrained.

The answer is sensible scale. Projects can begin on the campus or in nearby institutions. A school might maintain an accessible reading collection, support a supervised recycling system or help document local history with permission. The partner organisation should help decide the task, and teachers should review progress rather than count photographs. A short written reflection can connect experience to evidence and identify what needs changing.

Examination preparation remains important, but it is not the whole purpose of education. An afternoon of carefully chosen work can develop judgement, cooperation and responsibility alongside academic skills. The proposal deserves support when service is sustained, useful and accountable. Its success should be measured by what improves for the community and what students learn to do thoughtfully, rather than by how busy or charitable the school appears.''',
'''I decided that our new neighbour was unfriendly before I had heard her speak more than three words. Each afternoon she passed our doorway carrying a canvas bag. She returned my greeting briefly and never stopped to talk. When our residents’ group asked for help with a reading event, her name was absent from the volunteers’ list.

I complained about this to my mother while arranging donated books. She suggested that I take a poster upstairs rather than invent an explanation for someone I hardly knew. I went reluctantly, already preparing a polite speech for another refusal.

The neighbour opened the door with a finger against her lips. From the next room came a faint call, and she asked whether I could wait. I noticed a medicine chart beside the clock and a chair positioned close to the bedroom doorway. When she returned, she apologised and explained that her father needed almost constant care.

This time I listened. Her short afternoon walks were errands fitted between his meals and medical appointments. She had seen our notice but could not promise hours away from home. She asked whether there was work she could do upstairs while remaining nearby.

I took her a box of books with damaged labels. Over the next week she repaired them, made a clear catalogue and suggested a simple borrowing register. She had worked in a library for years. On the day of the event, the neatly organised collection was the first thing our teacher praised.

I felt pleased, then ashamed of the pleasure. I had almost excluded the most experienced person available because I treated a brief conversation as a complete account of her life.

After the event I returned to thank her. We talked quietly while she checked the repaired books. Nothing about her circumstances had become easy, and she still could not stop at every doorway. What had changed was my willingness to ask, wait and hear the answer before deciding what another person’s behaviour meant.''',
'''The notice on the library door gave us two weeks to remove everything from the lower shelves. A leaking pipe had damaged the wall, and repairs would close the room for a month. For our reading club, the timing seemed disastrous: the younger pupils’ story sessions were due to begin the following Monday.

At our first meeting, we spent twenty minutes explaining why the sessions would have to stop. Then our librarian asked a different question. “Which part of a library must stay inside this room?”

We looked at the shelves, the desks and the large issue register. The books needed protection, she explained, and borrowing needed a reliable record. Neither requirement demanded the same room every day. We began drawing up a smaller plan instead of waiting for the original one to become possible.

Each class agreed to hold one labelled box. Two sturdy tables in the covered corridor became an afternoon reading corner. We selected books carefully, keeping valuable or fragile volumes in secure storage. The principal approved the arrangement after checking that it left the passage clear.

Our first mobile session was untidy. A box arrived late, two pupils wanted the same book, and the wind scattered our loose paper slips. By the next day we had a clipboard, a borrowing timetable and a clear responsibility for returning every box. The younger children seemed more interested in the new arrangement than disappointed by the missing room.

When the library reopened, we were ready to carry everything back. Several pupils asked whether one corridor session could continue. Children who had never entered the library had begun borrowing because they encountered books on their normal route through school.

The repaired room remained important, but the interruption had changed our idea of its purpose. We had thought that keeping the library open meant keeping its door unlocked. Now we understood that access also depended on bringing books, records and willing readers into contact, wherever a safe and useful meeting place could be made.'''
]

LETTER_BODIES=[
('The Municipal Commissioner\nMunicipal Corporation\nRivapur','Unsafe traffic outside Sunrise School','I am a Class X student of Sunrise School on Station Road. Between 7:30 and 8:00 a.m., vehicles frequently stop beside the school gate, narrowing the carriageway. Pupils then have to cross between parked vehicles, while buses turning into the entrance obstruct their view.\n\nI request an inspection during the morning arrival period. A clearly marked pedestrian crossing, a short no-parking zone and supervised traffic movement would make crossing safer. The school can help by staggering bus arrivals and reminding families to use the designated drop-off point.\n\nPlease consider these measures in consultation with the school and local residents. I would appreciate information about the action proposed.','Yours faithfully,\nRiya Sen'),
('The Director\nCity Museum\nRivapur','Request for an educational visit','Our Class X history group would like to visit the City Museum on 22 January 2027. Thirty pupils and two teachers hope to study the local-history gallery as part of a project on the use of material evidence.\n\nWe propose arriving at 10:00 a.m. and leaving by noon. Kindly let us know whether a guided session is available, the admission charges and any restrictions on note-taking or photography. One pupil uses a wheelchair, so information about step-free access would help us make suitable arrangements.\n\nPlease confirm whether the proposed visit is convenient. We will follow the museum’s rules and send the final group details once permission has been received.','Yours faithfully,\nRiya Sen\nClass Representative'),
('The Manager\nFairplay Sports\nRivapur','Replacement of damaged sports equipment','On 6 January 2027, I received the badminton equipment ordered under invoice FP214. One racket has a cracked frame, and the shuttlecock container was crushed on delivery. The equipment has not been used.\n\nThe outer package showed damage when it arrived. I have retained the packaging and invoice and can provide photographs of the defects. The remaining racket appears undamaged.\n\nPlease arrange collection and replace the damaged items, or advise how a refund for those items can be obtained. As the equipment is needed for a school event on 20 January, an early response would be helpful. Kindly confirm the proposed remedy and any further information you require.','Yours faithfully,\nRiya Sen'),
('The Editor\nThe Rivapur Chronicle\nRivapur','Encouraging young readers to use the public library','Through your newspaper, I wish to draw attention to the declining use of our neighbourhood public library. Its quiet reading space and affordable access to books are particularly valuable to pupils who cannot maintain a large collection at home.\n\nSeveral classmates do not know its opening hours or how to obtain a membership card. Clear notices in schools, a simple online catalogue and an occasional orientation session could remove these practical barriers. Student reading groups and book discussions might help new members feel welcome.\n\nI hope the library authorities and local schools will consider a joint programme. Publicity should be followed by dependable opening hours and a collection that responds to readers’ interests.','Yours faithfully,\nRiya Sen'),
('The Principal\nSunrise School\nRivapur','Permission to organise a reading afternoon','The Class X reading club requests permission to organise a reading afternoon on 23 January 2027, from 2:00 to 3:30 p.m., in the library. We hope to introduce pupils of Classes VI and VII to the borrowing system and help them choose suitable books.\n\nClub members will prepare short readings and small discussion groups under the librarian’s supervision. Participation will be limited to thirty pupils so that normal library use is not disturbed. Preparation will take place during club periods, and no regular lessons need to be missed.\n\nKindly approve the event and advise whether any arrangements should be changed. We will submit the programme and volunteers’ responsibilities for review before issuing a notice.','Yours faithfully,\nRiya Sen\nReading Club Secretary'),
('Dear Grandfather,','', 'Last week I finally used the sewing skill you taught me during the holidays. A strap came loose from my school bag just as I was packing for an excursion. I first thought I would have to borrow another bag, but then I remembered your small repair kit.\n\nI matched the torn edges, held the strap securely and made several careful stitches. I worked slowly and checked that the needle had not caught the lining. The strap held throughout the trip.\n\nWhat pleased me most was being able to repair something instead of discarding it immediately. Thank you for insisting that I practise on an old cloth before touching the bag. I hope to show you the repair when we visit next month.','With love,\nRiya'),
('Dear Meera,','', 'I was sorry to hear that you were not selected for the basketball team. You had practised hard, so I understand why the result feels disappointing. You do not have to pretend that it does not matter.\n\nWhen I missed the debate shortlist last term, I initially stopped practising. Later, the teacher’s comments showed me exactly where I had lost clarity. Working on those points helped more than repeatedly comparing myself with the selected speakers.\n\nCould you ask your coach for two specific things to improve before the next trial? I would be happy to help you keep a practice record. Keep playing the game you enjoy, and allow yourself some time to recover from the disappointment. One selection need not decide what you can eventually achieve.','Yours affectionately,\nRiya'),
('Dear Kavya,','', 'You mentioned feeling worried about moving to Pune. I felt much the same before our move last year, especially about joining a class where everybody already seemed to know one another. The first few days were uncomfortable, but they did not remain that way.\n\nLearning the route to school and arranging my books before term began made mornings less confusing. Joining the library club gave me something to discuss with classmates besides the fact that I was new. I also kept in touch with old friends without expecting them to be available every evening.\n\nGive yourself time, and ask for help with small practical things. You need not make a complete new life in your first week. Write when you arrive; I would love to hear about your neighbourhood.','With love,\nRiya'),
('Dear Meera,','', 'Our school exhibition was more useful than I expected. I had assumed that visitors would simply admire finished models, but most wanted to know how the models worked and what their limitations were.\n\nOur group demonstrated a water filter made from simple materials. The water looked clearer after filtration, but we explained that this did not prove it was safe to drink. Several visitors asked about further treatment, which led to a lively discussion. I was glad that we had tested the model rather than memorised an impressive description.\n\nPreparing together also taught us to divide responsibilities fairly. Next time, I would keep a better record of each trial from the beginning. I hope your exhibition goes well. Tell me what your group is planning.','Yours affectionately,\nRiya'),
('Dear Anaya,','', 'I was delighted to hear about your interest in our reading club. It began with only eight members, but its most useful activity is quite simple: each member brings a short recommendation and explains who might enjoy the book.\n\nWe meet on Friday afternoons after lessons. Nobody is required to finish an unfamiliar book in a week, and members may disagree politely about what they have read. Last month we also helped younger pupils find books in the library. Their questions reminded us how confusing a large collection can seem at first.\n\nIf you start a similar group, keep the first meetings small and give everyone time to speak. I can send you our borrowing-record format and sample programme. I would love to hear which book your group chooses first.','With love,\nRiya')
]

LETTER_BODIES[8]=('Dear Meera,','','At our school exhibition yesterday, I unexpectedly had to supervise the entrance desk when a volunteer became unwell. I had prepared only for my own science demonstration, so the sudden change made me nervous.\n\nI first asked the teacher what decisions I could make. Then I separated visitors with bookings from those who needed information and asked a classmate to guide families to the hall. When a younger pupil could not find his group, I kept him at the desk and contacted the supervising teacher instead of letting him search alone.\n\nEverything eventually ran smoothly, but I learned that enthusiasm cannot replace clear instructions. Next time I would ask for an emergency contact list and a written allocation of duties before the event begins.','Yours affectionately,\nRiya')
LETTER_BODIES[9]=('Dear Didi,','','An experience this week changed my opinion of our new neighbour. I had assumed that she was unfriendly because she answered greetings briefly and never attended our residents’ activities.\n\nWhen I took her a poster for the reading event, I discovered that she was caring for her elderly father. She could not leave him for long, but she offered to repair book labels at home. Her careful catalogue became one of the most useful parts of the event.\n\nI realised how easily I had mistaken limited time for indifference. You often remind me to ask questions before judging people, and I finally understand your point. When you come home, I would like to discuss how we can involve neighbours whose responsibilities prevent them from joining ordinary meetings.','With love,\nRiya')

def enrich(bank):
    compositions=[q for q in bank if q['section'].startswith('Composition')]
    for q,model in zip(compositions,COMPOSITIONS):
        q['answer']='One complete model composition\n\n'+model+'\n\n'+q['answer']
        q['modelWords']=len(model.split())
    for q,(recipient,subject,body,closing) in zip([q for q in bank if q['section'].startswith('Letters')],LETTER_BODIES):
        formal=q['genre']=='Formal'
        model='12 Lake Road\nRivapur\n10 January 2027\n\n'+(recipient+'\n\nSubject: '+subject+'\n\nSir/Madam,\n\n' if formal else recipient+'\n\n')+body+'\n\n'+closing
        q['answer']='One complete model letter\n\n'+model+'\n\n'+q['answer']
    return bank
